{
  "case_id": "2001-00-795",
  "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument now in No. 00-795, John Ashcroft versus the Free Speech Coalition. Mr. Clement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: Mr. Chief Justice and may it please the court: In 1996 Congress updated the child pornography laws to keep pace with technological developments. Congress extended the existing prohibitions on visual depictions of actual children engaged in sexually explicit conduct to cover virtually indistinguishable images in material pandered as child pornography. These provisions are constitutional for the same basic reason that other laws prohibiting child pornography have been upheld."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Has any case, to your knowledge, been lost on that ground in which that was a substantial argument?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: I think there are at least two reasons for that differential treatment. One is with respect to age that's a fact that's uniquely verifiable and in a lot of contexts we recognize that somebody's age is something that one can verify."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Does it trouble you at all that the statute would prohibit a motion picture company from employing an 18-year-old actress to play the three roles that Justice Breyer identified?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0001-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And interestingly, if you look at footnote 20 in the Ferber opinion the Court went out of its way to invoke the general doctrine that you don't apply cases before they arise and you don't adopt a rationale any broader than necessary to decide the case before it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "O'Connor's question. Basically we're talking here about overbreadth and the government's argument seems to be that since there is a class of materials that we can proscribe, the fact that it's overly broad is the respondent's problem."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the government's argument seems to be that since there is a class of materials that we can proscribe, the fact that it's overly broad is the respondent's problem."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0001-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the virtually indistinguishable phrase already appears in the text of the statute, it just doesn't appear in the operative provision."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The petitioner's argument if adopted would have three radical tragic consequences for First Amendment jurisprudence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Sirkin",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0001-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "in 1995 a member of the Department of Justice testified before the Senate committee that heard evidence about this statute and indicated that there had been a conviction rate of over 97 percent in cases that had been brought against people for the possession or dissemination of material containing a depiction of a minor."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Sirkin",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0001-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It used the terminology, it created a category of computer images that would be nonreal or other pictures or images that are nonreal and that are fictional characters into the act and it would be prohibited if disseminated or possessed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Am I not correct in remembering that one of the amendments to this series of statutes by Congress specifically eliminated the requirement of patently offensive and the like?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What about the other argument which is look, we're trying to protect the real children who are the subjects of the film and the reason we need this to protect them is because there is no way for any expert or anyone else to say when they see a film whether this is a film of a real person or whether it is a fake film made through virtual technology. The experts just can't tell us."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Counsel for Respondent notes the 97 percent conviction figure."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e0609f71658fef7612e4b0d9df0c3fffed11f24367b810332df5e4bf9039df3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e0609f71658f-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the 1996 Act has allowed us to prosecute cases even where there is a reasonable doubt about whether it is a virtual image or not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2001-00-795",
      "confidence": 0.0,
